00:00:01.829 --> 00:00:01.839 align:start position:0%
domestic flights are about to get  cheaper  the chancellor has cut air passenger  duty a long planned move to bring taxes  on internal flights into line with  international ones  but for some it was a surprising  decision just days ahead of the crucial  cop 26 u.n climate summit being hosted  in glasgow there was an enormous  climate-shaped hole in the heart of this  budget and it was really very very  disappointing to see the few measures  that were there were actually going in  the wrong direction in other words  making car driving cheaper making short  haul flights cheaper so this was a  massively wasted opportunity and i think  demonstrates ahead of this climate  summit that this government is simply  not serious about showing any kind of  climate leadership  passengers at glasgow airport will be  saving six pounds fifty per flight think  differently i'm waiting for  my son to arrive with my granddaughter  so i quite  enjoy the fact that it's going to be a  little bit cheaper i'd love to travel  from london to glasgow on a on a train  but it does take  well it takes a day rather than an hour  for some airline managers struggling  with the effects of covid on travel the  tax cut is a welcome one what i think  this cut will do is to support a number  of routes particularly those around the  periphery of the united kingdom which  are facing increasing economic  challenges  although the government's net zero  carbon emissions strategy didn't feature  prominently in the budget the chancellor  has increased taxes on long-haul flights  over five and a half thousand miles  perhaps providing some balance  in many ways this is less about the  detail of counting the carbon emissions  than it is about the optics about the  message the uk government is sending to  the world about the sacrifices it is and  is not willing to make in the race to go  green  the glasgow summit is looming and the  deliberate choice to make some carbon  intensive transport cheaper will lead  many to question the climate credibility  of the uk at the very moment it is  trying to be a global leader  hannah thomas peter sky news
 

